    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'What evidence do I need to file for absence divorce?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'For an Article 79 absence divorce: original marriage certificate (attested, Arabic-translated), Emirates ID, evidence of husband\'s last known address or contact details, bank statements showing no maintenance payments received, records of communication attempts (WhatsApp, email, phone records), and any records of his departure (flight booking if known, last UAE entry/exit stamp). For financial abandonment under Article 72: the same documents plus evidence of the 3-month maintenance gap and that the husband had capacity to pay.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'What if my husband comes back after I filed?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'If your husband returns and appears at court after you have filed for absence divorce, the proceedings continue, they do not automatically stop. The husband has the right to contest the divorce and present his case. The court will then hear both sides and determine whether the grounds for divorce are established. If the husband pays the outstanding maintenance and can demonstrate a valid reason for his absence, the court may decline to grant the divorce. However, if you have suffered genuine abandonment, the court takes that into account.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Can my husband contest the divorce after disappearing?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Yes. A husband who was absent during proceedings can apply to contest a judgment issued in absentia within the appeal period (typically 30 days from when he becomes aware of the judgment). He would need to demonstrate both a valid reason for his absence and grounds to contest the divorce. UAE courts apply a high standard for reopening absence judgments, genuine emergency or inability to receive service is required. Deliberate avoidance of court notices does not qualify.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Will I get mahr if he abandoned me?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Yes. Mahr is your legal right regardless of who initiated the divorce and regardless of your husband\'s abandonment. A judicial divorce obtained through absence proceedings (faskh) does not extinguish the wife\'s mahr rights. The deferred mahr falls due upon the divorce judgment. If the husband is absent and has no known assets in the UAE, collecting the mahr may require enforcement through international judicial cooperation, which is possible but more complex than domestic enforcement. See the mahr enforcement guide for options.',
